    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d418">

                <pb facs="0490" n="484" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg484"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/26448: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in the Soviet Union</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Dickerson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-12-12T12:00:00+03:00">December 12 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >11</hi>], 1942—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received December 11—10:08
                        p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1045. Soviet press comment on the scuttling of the French Fleet at Toulon<anchor
                        n="73" xml:id="d418-f1"/> has been highly commendatory and has expressed
                    great satisfaction at its successful execution.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dickerson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d418-f1" n="73" xml:id="d418-f1-content">
                <p>Most of the French Fleet was scuttled on November 27, 1942, as German troops
                    entered Toulon. A few ships escaped to North African ports. Secretary of the
                    Navy Frank Knox announced on December 8 that about one quarter of the ships had
                    fallen intact and afloat into German control.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
